## std::stack in C++ — LIFO container adapter

`std::stack` is a container adapter (header `<stack>`) providing
Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) access. It does not implement its own storage;
by default it wraps `std::deque`, but can be templated over another
underlying container, e.g. `std::stack<int, std::vector<int>>`.

### Declaration
```cpp
#include <stack>
std::stack<int> s;
std::stack<char> s2;
std::stack<int, std::vector<int>> s3;  // custom underlying container
```

### Core operations
| Function        | Meaning                              |
|-----------------|----------------------------------------|
| push(val)       | Add element to top                     |
| pop()           | Remove top element — returns nothing   |
| top()           | Access top element (reference)         |
| empty()         | true if no elements                    |
| size()          | number of elements                     |
| emplace(args)   | construct element in-place on top      |

### Gotcha (important, non-obvious)
`pop()` does NOT return the removed value — it only removes it. To get
the value and remove it, call `top()` first, then `pop()` separately:
```cpp
char c = s.top();
s.pop();
```
Calling `top()` or `pop()` on an empty stack is undefined behavior —
always guard with `empty()` first.

### Worked example: Valid Parentheses (LeetCode)
```cpp
#include <stack>
#include <string>

bool isValid(std::string s) {
    std::stack<char> st;
    for (char c : s) {
        if (c == '(' || c == '[' || c == '{') {
            st.push(c);
        } else {
            if (st.empty()) return false;
            char top = st.top();
            st.pop();
            if ((c == ')' && top != '(') ||
                (c == ']' && top != '[') ||
                (c == '}' && top != '{')) {
                return false;
            }
        }
    }
    return st.empty();
}
```
This pattern (push opening brackets, pop-and-compare on closing brackets,
require empty stack at the end) is the canonical approach for bracket
matching / balanced parentheses problems.

## Project setup notes (DSA_Leetcode repo)

- `std::string` requires `#include <string>`.
- gtest is preinstalled system-wide on this machine (Ubuntu, libgtest-dev
  1.14.0): headers at `/usr/include/gtest`, compiled libs at
  `/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libgtest.a` and `libgtest_main.a`. No
  additional install needed.
- Compile+link a gtest binary with:
  ```
  g++ -std=c++17 <Test>.cpp <Impl>.cpp -lgtest -lgtest_main -pthread -o <out>
  ```
  Both the test file AND the implementation .cpp must be passed to g++ —
  omitting the implementation file causes `undefined reference` linker
  errors, since the header only declares the method.
- Fixed bug pattern seen in `PalindromeNumber.cpp`: method definitions
  outside the class must repeat the return type (`bool Solution::isPalindrome(int x)`,
  not `Solution::isPalindrome(int x)`), and local project headers must be
  included with quotes (`#include "PalindromeNumber.hpp"`), not angle
  brackets (`#include <PalindromeNumber.hpp>`) — angle brackets only search
  system/library include paths.
- This repo's wiki mode was set to `project` (`wiki use project`), so
  captures land in `DSA_Leetcode/wiki/` rather than the cross-project
  `~/wiki/`.
